  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Colorado has received $10.5B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 18,244 awards with an average award size of $577K. Obligations fell 34.6% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $6.1B in the state so far. SIERRA NEVADA COMPANY, LLC and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP are the largest vendors, with $1.9B and $1.7B in obligations respectively. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligations at $1.5B, followed by Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) at $1.3B. Fiscal-year trends show higher totals in prior years, with FY25 at $16.1B and FY24 at $15B. Current-year totals are partial because figures are fiscal year to date. This snapshot highlights concentration in defense and aerospace buying and a vendor base led by major prime contractors and specialized research and manufacturing firms.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did federal contracting in Colorado change year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 34.6% year over year. Colorado recorded $10.5B in FY2026 to date compared with higher totals in recent years, including $16.1B in FY25 and $15B in FY24. The number of awards was 18,244 with an average award size of $577K. Current-year totals are partial because figures are fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest agencies and vendors driving Colorado contract dollars?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $6.1B in obligations. SIERRA NEVADA COMPANY, LLC and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP are the largest vendors with $1.9B and $1.7B respectively. Other significant buyers include the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $925M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $838M. Aircraft Manufacturing leads industries with $1.5B in obligations."
      }
